\ pool.4 - buffers that live outside the image and are allocated on
\ first use.
\
\ Why: `CREATE name n ALLOT` reserves n bytes of *dictionary* at
\ compile time, so every byte ends up in a saved image whether or not
\ it is ever used. Measured on the first prebuilt shell image
\ (Iteration 40): 253,528 bytes total, of which 135,576 were CREATE'd
\ buffers and 77.3% of the whole file was zeros. One buffer,
\ POS-PARAMS-SAVE, was 73,728 bytes - 29% of the image - and entirely
\ empty. See PROGRESS.md's Iteration 41 entry.
\
\   BUFFER: ( size "name" --- )
\
\ declares a buffer of `size` bytes. The name behaves exactly like a
\ CREATE'd one - executing it pushes the buffer's address - so a call
\ site never changes. What changes is when and where the space comes
\ from:
\
\   - nothing but a three-cell descriptor goes in the dictionary, so a
\     saved image carries the declaration but not the space;
\   - the space is ALLOCATEd from the host heap on FIRST USE, so a
\     buffer that is declared but never touched costs nothing at all
\     at runtime either;
\   - the memory is outside the image entirely, so buffer sizes no
\     longer trade against dictionary space.
\
\ Descriptor layout, at the body address DOES> hands back:
\
\   +0  ptr     the ALLOCATEd address; 0 only between RESET-BUFFERS
\               and the next ALLOC-BUFFERS
\   +1  size    in bytes
\   +2  link    offset of the previously declared buffer's body, 0 at
\               the end of the chain - an OFFSET from START, not an
\               address, so it survives being saved and reloaded
\               (Iteration 40's lesson: anything stored or compiled
\               that names a location must be position-independent)
\
\ The pointer is at +0 deliberately: the whole runtime cost of a
\ buffer reference is then `DOES> @`, one fetch on top of what a
\ CREATE'd name costs. The original layout put the pointer at +2 and
\ allocated lazily, so every reference ran a store, three fetches, an
\ add and a branch - about ten threaded operations where CREATE costs
\ one. Iteration 129 measured that at 13% of the loop benchmark and
\ reverted the one conversion it had tried. Allocating eagerly and
\ putting the pointer first is what makes the conversion affordable.
\
\ A linked list rather than a table, so there is no fixed maximum
\ number of buffers to pick and get wrong.
\
\ The pointer is process-local and meaningless after a save/reload,
\ which is what RESET-BUFFERS is for: SAVE-SYSTEM calls it so an image
\ is written with every buffer back in its "not yet allocated" state.
\ That also means SAVE-SYSTEM discards buffer *contents* - fine for
\ its actual use (build a fresh image and exit) and documented rather
\ than silent.

BASE @  DECIMAL

VARIABLE BUF-LIST   0 BUF-LIST !
VARIABLE BUF-BODY
VARIABLE BUF-SIZE
VARIABLE BUF-PTR

: BUF-ZERO ( addr u --- )   \ u is a whole number of cells
  OVER + SWAP                          ( end addr )
  BEGIN 2DUP > WHILE 0 OVER ! CELL+ REPEAT
  2DROP ;

: BUF-ALLOC ( body --- )   \ give one descriptor its space
  DUP @ 0= IF
    \ Round up to a whole cell so BUF-ZERO can work a cell at a time
    \ without overrunning; a few bytes of slack per buffer is nothing
    \ against the heap and the sizes are not all cell multiples.
    DUP CELL+ @ CELL+ 1- [ 1 CELLS 1- ] LITERAL INVERT AND BUF-SIZE !
    BUF-SIZE @ ALLOCATE ABORT" BUFFER: out of memory"
    BUF-PTR !
    \ CREATE ... ALLOT space starts zeroed, so this must too. Done a
    \ cell at a time rather than with FILL, which is a per-BYTE
    \ threaded loop (kernel.4) - at ~34KB of buffers per boot that was
    \ measured as most of a 2.4x startup regression when the shell's
    \ buffers moved out of the image in Iteration 136.
    BUF-PTR @ BUF-SIZE @ BUF-ZERO
    BUF-PTR @ SWAP !
  ELSE DROP THEN ;

: BUFFER: ( size "name" --- )
  CREATE
    HERE BUF-BODY !
    0 ,                           \ pointer
    ,                             \ size
    BUF-LIST @ ,                  \ link to the previous declaration
    BUF-BODY @ START @ - BUF-LIST !
    BUF-BODY @ BUF-ALLOC          \ eagerly, so a reference is one fetch
  DOES> ( --- addr )
    @ ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Growing a buffer (Iteration 249)
\ ==================================================================
\ BUF-ENSURE gives a buffer at least u bytes, keeping its contents: a
\ new block of at least twice the old size, the contents copied over,
\ the descriptor updated. The old block is NOT freed. Code holds
\ absolute addresses into these buffers - shell.4's ARGV points into
\ its token buffers - and a freed block could be reused under them.
\ So the old block goes on a retired list, and stays readable exactly
\ as long as today's rule already says such a pointer is worth
\ anything: until the next command. BUF-FREE-RETIRED releases the list,
\ and must be called only where nothing can still point into a retired
\ block - shell.4 calls it between top-level commands. Doubling keeps
\ what is retired below the size of the buffer that replaced it.
\
\ The list nodes are separate two-cell blocks [next][block], so a
\ retired buffer's own bytes are never touched.

VARIABLE BUF-RETIRED   0 BUF-RETIRED !    \ first node, or 0

: BUF-ROUND ( u --- u' )   \ up to a whole number of cells
  CELL+ 1- [ 1 CELLS 1- ] LITERAL INVERT AND ;

: BUF-RETIRE ( addr --- )
  2 CELLS ALLOCATE ABORT" BUFFER: out of memory"   ( addr node )
  SWAP OVER CELL+ !
  BUF-RETIRED @ OVER !
  BUF-RETIRED ! ;

: BUF-FREE-RETIRED ( --- )
  BUF-RETIRED @
  BEGIN ?DUP WHILE
    DUP CELL+ @ FREE DROP
    DUP @ SWAP FREE DROP
  REPEAT
  0 BUF-RETIRED ! ;

: BUF-ENSURE ( u body --- )
\ Make the buffer described at body hold at least u bytes.
  2DUP CELL+ @ > 0= IF 2DROP EXIT THEN        \ already big enough
  SWAP OVER CELL+ @ 2* MAX BUF-ROUND          ( body n )
  DUP ALLOCATE ABORT" BUFFER: out of memory"  ( body n a )
  2DUP SWAP BUF-ZERO
  2 PICK @ ?DUP IF                            ( body n a old )
    DUP 2 PICK 5 PICK CELL+ @ MOVE
    BUF-RETIRE
  THEN
  2 PICK !  SWAP CELL+ ! ;

\ The two words shell code uses, both IMMEDIATE, taking the buffer's
\ name at compile time. They compile its descriptor as an offset from
\ START and add START back when they run: ['] would compile an
\ absolute address, which is wrong the moment a saved image reloads
\ somewhere else.
: BUF-BODY, ( "name" --- )
  ' >BODY START @ - POSTPONE LITERAL POSTPONE START POSTPONE @ POSTPONE + ;

: BUFFER-BODY ( --- body )  ( compiling: "name" --- )
  BUF-BODY, ; IMMEDIATE

: ENSURE-BUFFER ( u --- )   ( compiling: "name" --- )
  BUF-BODY, POSTPONE BUF-ENSURE ; IMMEDIATE

: BUFFER-SIZE ( --- u )     ( compiling: "name" --- )
  BUF-BODY, POSTPONE CELL+ POSTPONE @ ; IMMEDIATE

: ALLOC-BUFFERS ( --- )
  \ Give every declared buffer its space. Called at boot, because
  \ RESET-BUFFERS zeroed every pointer before the image was written -
  \ a heap address means nothing after a save and reload. A turnkey
  \ image must call this before touching any buffer; shell.4's MAIN
  \ does it first thing.
  BUF-LIST @
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
    START @ + DUP BUF-ALLOC
    2 CELLS + @
  REPEAT DROP ;

: RESET-BUFFERS ( --- )
  \ Release every allocated buffer and mark it unallocated again.
  \ Called by SAVE-SYSTEM before writing, so a saved image never
  \ contains a stale heap address - and, incidentally, so two builds
  \ of identical sources produce identical images.
  BUF-FREE-RETIRED
  BUF-LIST @
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
    START @ +                     ( body )
    DUP @ ?DUP IF FREE DROP THEN
    DUP 0 SWAP !
    2 CELLS + @                   ( link of the previous declaration )
  REPEAT DROP ;


BASE !
